     "type": "text",
     "text": "The exact value is **not known in general for \\(p\\ne2\\)**. This is the unresolved sharp-exponent problem for the matrix-weighted Hilbert transform.\n\nThe standard bounds, with the characteristic normalized as in the question, are\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\max\\!\\left\\{1,\\frac1{p-1}\\right\\}\n\\;\\le\\;\\beta(p)\\;\\le\\;\n1+\\frac1{p-1}-\\frac1p .}\n\\]\nThe lower bound follows already from scalar weights: taking \\(W(x)=w(x)I_2\\) reduces both the characteristic and the operator estimate to their scalar counterparts. The upper bound is the known general matrix-\\(A_p\\) estimate.\n\nFor comparison, at the excluded exponent \\(p=2\\), the upper bound is sharp:\n\\[\n\\beta(2)=\\frac32,\n\\]\nas shown by counterexamples to the matrix \\(A_2\\) conjecture, already in dimension two. That result does **not** establish sharpness of the displayed upper bound for \\(p\\ne2\\).\n\nThus an exact formula for the \\(p\\) specified in the question would require resolving an open problem.",
